    <h1>What Is a Murder of Crows? And an Unkindness of Ravens?</h1>
    <p class="id-subtitle">Medieval terms of venery — where they came from</p>
  </header>

  <section class="id-snippet" aria-label="Quick answer">
    <p><strong class="claim-lore">[lore]</strong> "Murder of crows" and "unkindness of ravens" are <strong>medieval collective nouns</strong> from the tradition of "terms of venery" — poetic group names for animals compiled in texts like the <em>Book of Saint Albans</em> (1486). They are not modern ornithological terms. Scientists simply say "flock" or "roost." The colourful names reflect medieval attitudes toward corvids but are not based in bird behaviour.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="id-section">
    <h2>Terms of Venery: The Source</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-lore">[lore]</strong> "Terms of venery" (from Latin <em>venatio</em>, hunting) were collective nouns for animals compiled in late medieval England. The most important source is the <em>Book of Saint Albans</em> (1486), attributed to Dame Juliana Berners, which contains the <em>Boke of St Albans</em> — a treatise on hunting, fishing, and heraldry including a large list of animal group names.</p>
    <p>These terms were partly practical, partly social — knowing them was a mark of education and courtly refinement. Many are fanciful, poetic, or based on folk attitudes rather than animal behaviour. They were always a minority usage; "flock" was the common word for bird groups in everyday speech.<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
  </section>

  <section class="id-section">
    <h2>Murder of Crows</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-lore">[lore]</strong> "A murder of crows" appears in later lists of terms of venery; it is not in the 1486 <em>Book of Saint Albans</em> itself, though it circulates widely as if it were. The term became popular in the 20th century, partly through its use in literature and popular culture.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>The word "murder" likely reflects medieval associations of crows with death and ill omen — carrion feeders at battlefields and execution sites</li>
      <li>It may also reflect folk beliefs about crows "judging" and "executing" one of their own (a "crow court" or "crow murder") — behaviour sometimes observed but not reliably documented as a consistent pattern</li>
      <li>In modern ornithology: a group of crows is simply a "flock"</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="id-section">
    <h2>Unkindness of Ravens / Conspiracy of Ravens</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-lore">[lore]</strong> "An unkindness of ravens" and "a conspiracy of ravens" are both cited as traditional collective nouns for ravens. The <em>Book of Saint Albans</em> (1486) lists "an unkindness of ravens."</p>
    <ul>
      <li>"Unkindness" may reflect the medieval belief that young ravens were callous to their parents (echoing the reference in Psalm 147:9 / Job 38:41 — God feeding the young ravens, perhaps implying the parents do not)</li>
      <li>"Conspiracy" reflects the mysterious and scheming quality attributed to ravens in medieval thought</li>
      <li>In modern ornithology: ravens gather in "flocks" or at large food sources in "roosts" or "assemblages"</li>
    </ul>
    <p>The existing question <a href="/questions/what-is-a-group-of-ravens-called/">What is a group of ravens called?</a> covers this topic in more detail.<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a>,<a href="#src-2">2</a></sup></p>
  </section>

  <section class="id-section">
    <h2>Other Corvid Group Names</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-lore">[lore]</strong> For completeness, other corvid terms of venery include:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Rooks:</strong> a "parliament" or "building" of rooks</li>
      <li><strong>Magpies:</strong> a "tiding" (or "tittering" or "gulp") of magpies</li>
      <li><strong>Jackdaws:</strong> a "clattering" of jackdaws</li>
      <li><strong>Jays:</strong> a "party" or "scold" of jays</li>
    </ul>
    <p>All these terms have the same medieval origin and are largely unused in scientific contexts. They persist in popular usage, trivia, and writing about nature.<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
  </section>

